  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "How many ways can the letters of the word MENDELEV be arranged so that at least two of the E’s are together?",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q6a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p13-q6a-proposal",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Bob proposes that grouping two of the E’s means you can treat the word like MENDELV.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p13-q6a-count",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Therefore, there are ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\dfrac{7!}{2!}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " ways.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p13-q6a-response",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Is this correct, and why?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. The EE block and the single E are different elements, so division by $2!$ is invalid.",
          "worked": "The EE block and the single E are distinct elements, so swapping them can change the arrangement. They cannot be treated as two identical E’s. Also, treating EE as a block counts arrangements with EEE together twice, so simply removing the division by $2!$ does not give the required count.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q6b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Mary suggests to find the number of ways to arrange the letters without restriction, then subtract the number of ways the E’s are all separate.\n\nIs this correct?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. Subtracting arrangements with all E’s separate leaves exactly those with at least two E’s together.",
          "worked": "Every arrangement either has all three E’s separate or has at least two E’s together. Subtracting the first group from the unrestricted total gives the required count.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q6c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the number of arrangements.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4320$ arrangements.\n\nSubtract the arrangements with all E’s separate from $8!/3!$.",
          "worked": "There are eight letters, with three identical E’s.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{\\text{total}}&=\\frac{8!}{3!}\\\\N_{\\text{total}}&=6720\\end{align*}$$\n\nArrange M, N, D, L and V in $5!$ ways. They create six gaps. Place the three E’s in distinct gaps, dividing by $3!$ because the E’s are identical.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{\\text{separate}}&=\\frac{5!\\times6\\times5\\times4}{3!}\\\\N_{\\text{separate}}&=2400\\\\N_{\\text{required}}&=6720-2400\\\\N_{\\text{required}}&=4320\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
